  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dangerous heat and humidity will impact \nsoutheast Georgia and southeast South Carolina today. Extreme \nHeat Warnings and Heat Advisories are in effect for portions of \nthe forecast area.\n\nA strong mid-level ridge will prevail over the Rockies and \nPlains, with the eastern periphery building into the \nsoutheastern states. A weak front will linger in the vicinity of\nthe forecast area today. With the strong ridging aloft and the \nweak surface feature, temperatures will soar into the mid to \nupper 90s today. Dew points are forecast to reach into the mid \nto upper 70s, highest along the coastal corridor behind the sea \nbreeze. This set up will yield heat index values in the 110-115 \nrange east of I-95 and 105-110 just inland of I-95. Locations \nfar inland will likely see heat index values top out around 103.\nAn Excessive Heat Warning has been issued for areas east of \nI-95 and south of Beaufort County, SC, with a Heat Advisory in \neffect north of Beaufort and just west of I-95. Heat and \nhumidity will drop to near normal values towards the end of the \nweek as a front approaches the region. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Isolated to scattered thunderstorms are expected\nto develop across southeast Georgia this afternoon and evening.\nThere will be a risk of a few strong to severe storms capable \nof producing damaging wind gusts, primarily along and south of \nInterstate 16. \n\nA front is expected to gradually push southward today, becoming\naligned across portions of southeast GA by the afternoon and \nevening. Along and south of the front, isolated to scattered \nthunderstorms are expected to develop. There is good agreement \namong the hi-res models and the 29/00z HREF that the best chance\nof thunderstorm coverage will be along and south of I-16, and \neven moreso along and south of the Altamaha. The environment in \nthis area is progged to have MLCAPE values upwards of 2,000 J/kg\nand DCAPE values of 1,000 J/kg or higher. Also, model soundings\ndepict mid- level flow of around 25-30 knots. So the main \nthreat will certainly be damaging wind gusts. As mentioned \nbefore the risk of thunderstorms and the severe potential \nincreases the further south you go, especially for the counties \nalong and near the Altamaha. To realize the severe threat, it \nmight require storms to develop south of the Altamaha and then \noutflow boundaries to propagate north of the river and help to \ninitiate new convection. The main time period of concern is \nroughly from 4-10pm. This area continues to be highlighted in a \nMarginal Risk in SPC's Day 1 Severe Weather Outlook.",
